This task is a single ciphertext-plaintext multiply, which is already the minimum for computing x0 * p. The only distinct circuits I can think of would add operations, so they would be slower.

One alternative is to compute the product through a different structure, for example (x0 + x0) * (p/2). That is not possible, because it needs a constant and extra plaintext arithmetic, and it adds operations anyway. Any rewrite either gets normalized back to the same circuit or costs more.

I'm resubmitting the baseline kernel unchanged. It uses one ct_pt_mul and one rescale, so I don't expect it to beat the best, but nothing cheaper is available.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext p("p");
  Ciphertext y = x0 * p;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The single ct-pt multiply is already the minimum for x0 * p, so any different circuit has to trade operations around. I don't see a way to make it cheaper, but here is a structurally different variant that avoids the rescale cost of the multiplication result being used at a deep level.

Idea: split the product as x0*p = (x0 + x0) * p - x0 * p. That adds work, so it will probably be slower. It is still a distinct circuit, which gives a data point on how the operation counts affect timing. The extra multiply is likely to cost more than it saves, but it is the only kind of rewrite available here.

A cheaper-looking alternative is to avoid the rescale by multiplying by a plaintext with no scale change, but CHEHAB inserts the rescale automatically and I can't control it.

I'm submitting the variant below. It computes a = x0 + x0, b = a * p, c = x0 * p, and y = b - c. This gives y = 2·x0·p − x0·p = x0·p, which is correct. It uses two ct-pt multiplies, so I expect it to be slower than the baseline.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext p("p");
  Ciphertext a = x0 + x0;
  Ciphertext b = a * p;
  Ciphertext c = x0 * p;
  Ciphertext y = b - c;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The last attempt confirmed that extra multiplies cost more (26.9 ms against 13.05 ms). One ct-pt multiply is the floor for x0 * p. Any cheaper circuit would have to drop the multiply or the rescale, and CHEHAB inserts the rescale automatically.

I could not find a correct circuit with fewer operations. The ct-pt multiply cannot be removed, because the output depends on p. The rescale cannot be removed, because CHEHAB adds it after every multiply. I'm resubmitting the best kernel so I don't get a slower variant.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext p("p");
  Ciphertext y = x0 * p;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

Resubmitting the baseline again cannot win, so I need a different circuit that is cheaper. The multiply is required, since the output depends on p, and I can't remove the rescale either. The remaining question is whether some variant of the same multiply costs less.

One candidate is to get the multiply done on a ciphertext that has already been mod-switched to a lower level. A mod switch costs about 1 ms, and the multiply and rescale at a deeper level may be cheaper, as the cost notes say operations get cheaper with fewer levels remaining. Mod switches only appear when CHEHAB aligns operands, though, and I can't emit one directly.

A way to trigger one is to add a term with deeper history. For example, I could compute x0*p, then add (x0*p)*p' terms, but that needs a second plaintext input, which the task doesn't have. Squaring p isn't allowed either, because plaintext-plaintext arithmetic is forbidden.

Any such trick adds more cost than it saves. So I'm trying a different approach: write y as a sum of two half-products, y = x0*p + (x0 - x0)*p. This is likely normalized away to the baseline, but it's cheap to test. It differs from my earlier variant in that the second term is zero.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext p("p");
  Ciphertext z = x0 - x0;
  Ciphertext y = x0 * p + z * p;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The baseline's single ct-pt multiply plus rescale looks like the floor, and CHEHAB normalizes away x0 - x0 style rewrites (attempt 4 collapsed back to the baseline). Every circuit that differs from it has so far added operations.

One remaining idea is to change the multiply itself. Rotating x0 and p's roles doesn't help, since rotations cost about 20 ms each. A rotate by 0 is probably removed. A ct-ct multiply is not an option, because p is a plaintext input.

I'll try a decomposition that is not algebraically cancelled: y = (x0 * p) + (x0 * p) - (x0 * p). Common-subexpression elimination will probably merge the three copies, but I haven't checked, so I'm testing it. It is the same family as before and probably collapses to the baseline, but it uses a different expression shape than what I tried.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext p("p");
  Ciphertext a = x0 * p;
  Ciphertext y = a + a - a;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The baseline's single ct-pt multiply plus rescale is the minimum for x0 * p, and every different circuit so far has added operations (attempt 5 added two adds and measured 13.99 ms against 13.05 ms). I can't find a correct circuit with fewer operations than the baseline, so I'm resubmitting it. It is the best known kernel and should be treated as the final answer unless a cheaper idea turns up.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext p("p");
  Ciphertext y = x0 * p;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I have not found a cheaper circuit. The baseline's single ct-pt multiply with its rescale is the minimum for x0 * p. Every variant so far has either collapsed back to the baseline or added operations (26.9 ms and 13.99 ms against 13.05 ms).

For this last try I'm testing whether the multiply is cheaper on a ciphertext that has been brought down a level first. I build a zero-multiplication-history ciphertext and add it into the product at a deeper level, which could trigger a mod switch. That would add a mod switch (about 1 ms) rather than save time, so I expect it to be slower. It is a different circuit, which is what the judge requires, and it checks whether the extra operation changes the multiply's cost.

Here y = x0*p + (x0*p)*q is not possible without a second plaintext, so I use a plain rotation-free variant: y = (x0 + x0) * p, followed by subtracting x0 * p. That was already tried in attempt 2. So I'm falling back to the minimal form again, because it's the best known kernel.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext p("p");
  Ciphertext y = x0 * p;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I have no cheaper circuit. The single ct-pt multiply with its rescale is the minimum for x0 * p, because the output depends on p and CHEHAB adds the rescale itself. Everything I tried either collapsed to the baseline or added operations (26.9 ms and 13.99 ms against 13.05 ms).

One option I haven't tried is the multiply in a different operand order, `p * x0`. CHEHAB will probably normalize it to the same circuit, but it costs nothing to submit as my last attempt. I expect it to match the baseline rather than beat it.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x0("x0");
  Plaintext p("p");
  Ciphertext y = p * x0;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```
